// Surface fold-in — typed holes in PATTERN position.
//
// GRADUATION: the `?` (anonymous) and `?name` (named) hole spelling is now a
// pattern atom as well as an expression atom, and it is the SAME token pair in
// both places. What the rule buys is the sort: without it the melder still
// admitted a `?` in a pattern slot, but admitted the EXPRESSION mold, so the
// committed tile recorded `Sort::Expression` and every sort-directed consumer
// read a pattern hole as an expression that had wandered into pattern position.
// This fixture is the parser-side witness that the whole pattern grammar keeps
// molding cleanly around the new atom: a hole stands alone as an arm pattern,
// nests inside a constructor pattern, sits on either side of an or-pattern,
// carries an as-binder, and never absorbs the `=>` / `,` / `)` / `}` that
// closes the form around it.
//
// The hole SEMANTICS in pattern position — a branch carrying one is neither
// satisfied nor refuted, and a match whose residue only a hole could cover is
// possibly-inexhaustive rather than inexhaustive — belong to the live pattern
// analysis, not to the parser.
//
// Parse-only, like the rest of the surface tree, and for a reason this fixture
// should say out loud: a `case` arm whose pattern is a hole does NOT lower. The
// declared-data arm reader still requires a constructor-pattern shape and drops
// the arm, so the analysis describes an arm that never runs. gandr-l1cw is the
// bead that graduates this file to a runnable `model/` example: it lowers and
// evaluates such an arm, so the match is stuck on the hole's indeterminacy
// rather than on a dropped arm, and filling the hole resumes from the
// checkpoint.

data Color : Type {
  Red : Color;
  Green : Color;
  Blue : Color;
}

data Maybe(a : Type) : Type {
  Nothing : Maybe(a);
  Just : (x : a) --> Maybe(a);
}

// A bare pattern hole as a whole arm: the `=>` stays a sibling.
def anonymous = case c { ? => 0 };

// A named pattern hole: the name attaches to the hole, the `=>` stays a
// sibling.
def named = case c { ?arm => 0 };

// A pattern hole nested inside a constructor pattern: the `)` closes the
// constructor, not the hole.
def nested = case m { Just(?) => 0, Nothing => 1 };

// A named hole nested one level deeper still.
def deep = case m { Just(Just(?inner)) => 0, Nothing => 1 };

// Pattern holes on both sides of an or-pattern.
def alternation = case c { ? | ?other => 0 };

// A hole under an as-binder: the binder names whatever the hole comes to test.
def bound = case c { ? as whole => 0 };

// A hole beside ordinary branches, with the `,` staying a flat sibling.
def mixed = case c { Red => 0, ? => 1, Blue => 2 };

// A pattern hole in a binding statement's binder position.
def in_binder = thunk { val ? = 3; ret 0 };

// Expression holes and pattern holes in one match: the two positions share the
// spelling and nothing else.
def both_positions = case ? { Just(x) => ?body, ? => 0 };
